{
 "name": "clipboard_parity",
 "face_ring_start": "compared",
 "description": "Copy / cut / paste on open geometry: whether shared vertices are duplicated once or per-face, whether a cut leaves the vertices its surviving neighbours still need, and what a paste lands on top of itself.",
 "source": "frozen reference capture (task 1121, nine-tool sweep)",
 "provenance": {
  "schema": 1,
  "source": "live-capture",
  "reference": "ref-editor@11.2v3",
  "method": "command",
  "captured_utc": "2026-08-18",
  "harness": "tools/local/capture (tasks 1121/1123)",
  "task": "1160",
  "notes": "The shared-vertex question is the point of the two-quad and whole-fan cells: a pole hub belongs to every face of the fan, so a per-face duplication and a per-selection duplication give different vertex counts. Cutting half a fan asks the mirror question -- the hub must survive because the OTHER half still uses it. CHANNELS: the sweep's comparison was never vertex-only -- it compared the vertex multiset, the edge multiset, faces BOTH as ordered rings (winding shows) and as unordered position sets, the material partition, the per-face 'did this face's own tag change', and the post-command selection. Re-checked independently over all 215 frozen cases: ZERO agree on vertices while differing on ordered face rings, so nothing frozen here is a vertex-channel-only agreement. PRECISION: the sweep compared at 1e-4 and this fixture's tolerance matches it. The actual reproduction is far tighter -- 208 of the 215 cases are bit-identical at the dumps' 6 decimals and the remaining 7 agree to 3e-6 or better, that being the worst deviation anywhere in the set. The tolerance is the MEASUREMENT's bound, not the data's."
 },
